/**
 * @defgroup pump_efficiency_calculator Achievable Average and Maximum Pump Efficiency
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Combines empirical pump-efficiency corrections with a flow-based deviation factor.
 * @details
 * What average and maximum pump-efficiency estimates correspond to this pump style and duty?
 * These estimates support comparisons when a measured performance curve is unavailable. They do not
 * establish the actual efficiency, condition or suitability of an installed pump, and they do not select
 * an operating point or optimize rotational speed.
 *
 * @heading{Final Results and Interpretation}
 * Both returned values are dimensionless efficiency fractions. The average estimate includes viscosity
 * and specific-speed corrections. The maximum estimate multiplies that average by an empirical deviation
 * factor based on liquid flow:
 * @formula{pump-efficiency-results;
 *   \eta_{average}=\eta_{optimal},\qquad
 *   \eta_{max}=\eta_{average}D(Q)
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{average}; Returned corrected average pump-efficiency estimate; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\eta_{optimal}; Corrected efficiency from the style and duty calculation; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\eta_{max}; Returned maximum pump-efficiency estimate; \unitless}
 * @symrow{D; Flow-based deviation multiplier; \unitless}
 * @symrow{Q; Liquid flow in the US gallon convention; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * For example, average = 0.8393 means approximately 83.93%, and max = 0.8696 means approximately
 * 86.96%. The word maximum identifies this empirical estimate; it is not a guarantee, a measured
 * best-efficiency point, or a universal physical bound. Values are not clamped to 0–1.
 *
 * @heading{Follow the Duty Through the Estimate}
 * Supply pump style, rotational speed in rpm, liquid kinematic viscosity in cSt, stage count,
 * liquid flow in US gpm and total head in ft. All values are required and remain in those conventions.
 * The corrected-efficiency calculation first chooses a style/flow curve, then accounts for viscosity
 * and specific speed. Only after that calculation does the flow-based deviation multiplier determine
 * the maximum estimate. Intermediate values remain unrounded.
 *
 * @formula{pump-efficiency-average;
 *   \eta_{average}=\left(\frac{E_{pre}}{100}\right)C_{\nu}-C_{Ns}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{average}; Corrected average pump-efficiency estimate; \unitless}
 * @symrow{E_{pre}; Style/flow efficiency before corrections; \percent}
 * @symrow{100; Percentage-point scale per unit fraction; \percent}
 * @symrow{C_{\nu}; Viscosity multiplier; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C_{Ns}; Specific-speed efficiency decrement; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The percent-to-fraction conversion applies only to the pre-correction curve value. The speed correction
 * is already a fractional decrement and is subtracted, rather than multiplied. Multiplying the final
 * average by D does not repeat the viscosity or specific-speed corrections.
 *
 * The supporting methods and their coefficients are documented in
 * @ref optimal_pre_pump_efficiency_calculator, @ref optimal_specific_speed_calculator,
 * @ref optimal_specific_speed_correction_calculator, @ref optimal_pump_efficiency_calculator and
 * @ref optimal_deviation_factor_calculator. Specific speed uses total head divided by the supplied
 * stage count; there is no double-suction flow-halving adjustment. Vertical turbine uses its own
 * speed-correction table; axial flow uses the nonvertical speed-correction table even though it shares
 * the vertical-turbine pre-efficiency flow curves.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example: ANSI/API Pump Duty}
 * Consider an ANSI/API end-suction pump at 2000 rpm and 2000 US gpm, with 137 ft total head,
 * one stage and kinematic viscosity 1.107 cSt. The upper ANSI/API flow interval gives Epre about
 * 83.9708%. The viscosity parameter is about 0.3291, so the viscosity multiplier is capped at 1.
 * Specific speed is about 2233.596 in the US customary convention, and the corresponding decrement
 * is about 0.000372. The flow-based deviation factor is about 1.03602.
 *
 * @formula{pump-efficiency-example-average;
 *   \eta_{average}\approx\left(\frac{83.9708}{100}\right)\times1-0.000372
 *   \approx0.8393
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{average}; Example average estimate; \unitless}
 * @symrow{83.9708; Example pre-correction efficiency; \percent}
 * @symrow{100; Percentage-point scale per unit fraction; \percent}
 * @symrow{1; Example viscosity multiplier; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.000372; Example specific-speed decrement; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @formula{pump-efficiency-example-maximum;
 *   \eta_{max}\approx0.83934\times1.03602\approx0.8696
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{max}; Example maximum estimate; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.83934; Example average before display rounding; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1.03602; Example flow-based deviation multiplier; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Thus the duty estimates are approximately 83.93% average and 86.96% maximum. Calculations use
 * unrounded helper results; the displayed intermediate values above explain the method rather than
 * defining numerical rounding within it.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions, Boundaries and Inappropriate Uses}
 * These are empirical estimates with no validated operating range established here. Flow, viscosity,
 * rotational speed and head must use the stated unit conventions. Fractional stage counts are evaluated
 * numerically; accepting one does not establish that it describes a real pump. No input validation,
 * defaulting, interpolation across curve boundaries or output clamping is added.
 *
 * Specified-optimal-efficiency, positive-displacement and unknown native styles receive zero from the
 * pre-efficiency curve selector. The remaining corrections still apply, so their average and maximum
 * estimates can be negative. No supplied-efficiency override or positive-displacement performance model
 * is provided by this calculation. Do not interpret these fallbacks as measured performance.
 *
 * Zero, negative, extreme and nonfinite inputs retain the supporting methods' arithmetic outcomes.
 * For example, zero flow can combine a finite negative corrected average with an infinite deviation
 * factor and return a negative-infinite maximum. Negative flow can return a NaN maximum. The viscosity
 * and speed methods retain their fmin/fmax handling of nonfinite intermediate values, so an invalid
 * input need not yield NaN in every returned field. Such results do not represent usable pump duties.
 * Use appropriate pump data and engineering review before making equipment or operating decisions.
 *
 * @see pump_efficiency::calculate
 */
